      "latency_ms": 2793.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 559,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through chemical, physical, and ecological lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water consists of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The water we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which forms glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling water.\n\nWater also has remarkable physical traits:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients throughout our bodies.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water, allowing marine life to survive under frozen lakes in the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat, which helps regulate the temperature of the Earth's climate and the human body.\n\n### 3. Role on Earth\n* **Global Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through evaporation, condensation, and precipitation (rain and snow). The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 4. Role in Life\nWater is the foundation of all known life. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It is required for nearly every bodily function, including regulating temperature, flushing out waste, and protecting organs.\n* **Ecosystems:** Plants need water to perform photosynthesis (making their food), and countless animals and plants live in aquatic habitats.\n\n**In short:** Water is a simple chemical combination of hydrogen and oxygen, but it is also the miraculous, life-giving force that makes planet Earth unique."
